---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import TopNav from '@ghritam/ui/TopNav.astro';
import Footer from '@ghritam/ui/Footer.astro';
import PahadiFrieze from '@ghritam/ui/PahadiFrieze.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Philosophy"
  description="Why we started Ghritam — a letter from the founder.">
  <TopNav active="Philosophy" variant="minimal" />

  {/* ── Header ── */}
  <header class="px-5 pb-5 pt-10 text-center md:px-10 lg:px-40 lg:pt-[60px]">
    <svg
      width="260" height="44" viewBox="0 0 260 44" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
      stroke-width="1" class="mx-auto mb-3.5 text-ink opacity-85">
      <path d="M0 36 L30 18 L50 28 L80 8 L120 30 L160 14 L200 30 L230 20 L260 36" />
      <path d="M72 12 L80 8 L88 12" opacity="0.5" />
      <path d="M154 18 L160 14 L166 18" opacity="0.5" />
      <line x1="0" y1="36" x2="260" y2="36" />
      <path d="M124 36 V28 Q124 24 130 24 Q136 24 136 28 V36 Z" />
      <path d="M122 24 L138 24 L135 20 L125 20 Z" />
      <circle cx="130" cy="18" r="1.4" fill="currentColor" />
    </svg>
    <div class="font-dev text-accent" style="font-size:clamp(28px,5vw,40px)">दर्शन</div>
    <h1 class="mt-1.5 font-serif italic" style="font-size:clamp(32px,6vw,48px);line-height:1.1">
      Why we started Ghritam.
    </h1>
    <div class="mt-3.5 font-mono text-xs uppercase text-ink-3" style="letter-spacing:0.3em">
      A letter from the founder · 4 min read
    </div>
  </header>

  {/* ── Narrative body — single column, warm voice ── */}
  <article
    class="px-5 pb-10 font-serif text-ink md:px-12 lg:px-[200px]"
    style="font-size:18px;line-height:1.7">
    <p class="mb-4">
      <span
        class="float-left pr-2 font-dev text-accent"
        style="font-size:54px;line-height:0.85">न</span>
      amaste. If you are reading this, you probably already sense something is off
      with the food on our shelves. It looks the same every week. It tastes the
      same every month. It is quietly <em>fortified</em>, or <em>preserved</em>,
      or — sometimes — simply adulterated.
    </p>
    <p class="mb-4">
      Most food you can buy today has been modified. What is hard to find is food
      in its natural form — inconsistent from batch to batch, changing with the
      season, and yet still the best your body has ever known.
    </p>
    <p class="mb-4">
      <strong>Ask yourself this:</strong> if food were only vitamins and minerals,
      why would nature have given us this much diversity? So many fruits, so many
      palates, so many smells? If amla were only <em>Vitamin C</em> and honey only
      sucrose, a factory could have replaced the tree and the bee long ago. It has
      not.
    </p>
    <p class="mb-4">
      Your body is nature. It was not designed to be fed by a factory. It was
      designed to be fed by a forest, a farm, a hive. And behind each of those is a
      human — a farmer, a beekeeper, a villager — whose livelihood holds up the
      food chain of this country.
    </p>
    <p class="mb-4">
      Ghritam is our small attempt to bring those humans and their food into your
      kitchen, unchanged. That is the whole idea.
    </p>
  </article>

  <PahadiFrieze />
  <Footer topRule />
</Layout>
